# Docker build context for the native C++ build (docker/build.Dockerfile).
#
# The repo is ~2.8 GB on disk. The two monsters are:
#   content/art          1.2 GB   (authored art / Kenney packs)
#   .claude/worktrees    1.6 GB   (parallel agent checkouts, nested inside the repo)
# Neither is needed to COMPILE. The C++ build needs source, CMake files and
# nothing else; content/ is read at RUNTIME by the natively-run binary straight
# out of the repo, so it never has to enter the build context.
#
# This file is therefore an ALLOWLIST: deny everything, then re-admit the few
# directories the compiler actually reads. That keeps the context in the low
# hundreds of KB and, more importantly, keeps it that way when someone drops
# another gigabyte of art into the tree.

**

# --- re-admitted: the only things the build actually compiles ---
!native/**
!docker/**

# --- re-admitted: the baked worlds the content tests read ---
# granadad-content's tests are not unit tests over synthetic bytes; they open
# the owner's actual shipped .trojsav files and assert against facts read out of
# them. Without these three files in the context the content tests cannot run,
# and the build gate shrinks to "fixed.hpp compiles" while still printing green.
#
# This is 21 KB of baked worlds, not the 1.2 GB art tree. content/art and
# everything else under content/ stay out — only maps/baked comes in.
# Cost measured at the time of writing:
#   compound_block.trojsav    2,051 B
#   docks_surface.trojsav    17,695 B
#   tavern_fixture.trojsav      845 B
!content/maps/baked/**

# --- re-admitted: the custom tile pack the renderer draws with ---
# 43 KB of it: art-mapping.json (13 KB) and tiles.png (29 KB), the owner's
# original 16x16 pixel art in the MERCOLAS-24 palette.
#
# This is the ONE piece of content/art the build takes, and it earns its place
# for the same reason content/maps/baked does: without it the renderer's tests
# can only exercise the procedural fallback, so the gate would be proving that
# the code paths nobody ships still work. With it, every build loads the real
# sheet, binds every material to a real region, and renders a real frame of the
# real Docks.
#
# Everything else under content/art -- the Kenney packs, the faces, the sprite
# sets, 1.2 GB of it -- stays out and is read at runtime from the repo.
!content/art/custom/**

# --- re-admitted: the actor sprites the ward is drawn with (#78) ---
# 30 KB of it: sprite-index.json (14 KB) and sprites.png (16 KB), the owner's
# twenty-five 16x16 actor figures in the same MERCOLAS-24 palette, with the tag
# vocabulary and the per-type queries already authored.
#
# It earns its place for exactly the reason art/custom does. ActorSheet::load
# falls back to procedural silhouettes when the pack is absent -- a content edit
# must not stop the game booting -- and that fallback is precisely why the real
# files have to be here: without them every case about how the district's six
# hundred people LOOK would pass against a fallback that never ships.
!content/art/sprites/**

# --- re-admitted: the spell raws the priest of the Flame teaches from ---
# 11 KB, one file. S2's priest offers what the owner actually wrote, and the
# whole reason that matters is the standing direction on magic: effects have to
# stay MODULAR -- a spell in these raws is a list of components, not a baked
# outcome -- so spellcrafting can compose them later. A priest teaching out of a
# table baked into a .cpp would be a priest teaching something nobody authored,
# and the test that pins his offer would be pinning a fiction.
#
# It comes in for the same reason maps/baked and art/custom do: without it the
# case that reads the raws either fails or, worse, quietly passes against the
# empty-book fallback.
!content/raws/spells/**

# --- re-admitted: the raws the ward SPEAKS out of (S3) ---
# 100 KB, five files. The conversation layer writes no dialogue: every line in
# the game comes out of barks.json under an authored key, every named person out
# of notables.json, every story out of histories.json, and who may repeat which
# story out of rumors.json. skills.json owns the list of things there are to be
# good at, including the streetwise a haggle is fought with.
#
# They come in for exactly the reason spells.json does, and the first S3 build
# proved the point: without them BarkTables::load returns an empty set, every
# lookup returns nothing, and the whole social suite either fails or -- far
# worse -- passes against the empty-content fallback while the gate prints
# green. The loaders are deliberately silent about a missing file so the game
# still boots during a content edit, and that silence is exactly why the raws
# have to be in the context where a test can catch it.
#
#   barks/barks.json      59 KB   210 tables, 605 rows
#   names/notables.json   23 KB   the Forty (42, with Sedge and Haddie)
#   names/histories.json   7 KB   15 feuds, debts, romances, pacts, secrets
#   names/names.json       5 KB   the authored name pools
#   rumors/rumors.json     5 KB   who may repeat which story
#   skills/skills.json    10 KB   the 20-skill vocabulary
!content/raws/barks/**
!content/raws/names/**
!content/raws/rumors/**
!content/raws/skills/**

# --- re-admitted: the raws the ward's GUILDS come out of (S4) ---
# 12 KB, three files, and they are in the context for the same reason every
# other raws directory above is: the loaders are deliberately silent about a
# missing file so the game still boots during a content edit, and that silence
# is exactly what would let a whole suite pass against an empty registry.
#
#   factions/factions.json   2 KB   the owner's five factions and their jobs
#   factions/ranks.json      4 KB   S4: a ladder hung off each of those five
#   quests/*.json            6 KB   the owner's vanished-clerk line, which this
#                                   build skips by shape, and the Priest of the
#                                   Flame line, which it runs
#
# quests/ comes in whole rather than by filename, because QuestBook::load reads
# the DIRECTORY and tells the two schemas apart by shape. Admitting one file and
# not the other would make the case that proves it skips the owner's file pass
# for the wrong reason.
!content/raws/factions/**
!content/raws/quests/**

# --- re-admitted: the radiant contract board (S6) ---
# 8 KB, one file, and it is in for the strongest version of the reason above:
# every broker, patron and source it names is REFUSED AT LOAD unless the
# owner's notables.json has that id. Without the file in the context the board
# loads empty, every contract case passes against nothing, and the claim that a
# generated job can only ever name somebody who exists would be proved by an
# absence.
!content/raws/contracts/**

# --- re-admitted: the roll, and the households on it (S7) ---
# 8 KB, two files, and both are here for the reason every entry above is: a
# loader that is silent about a missing file lets a whole suite pass against
# nothing.
#
#   compounds/compounds.json   the five plots of DOCKS-GAZETTEER section 2.8 --
#                              C1-C4 and the glebe -- with their Den Dukes,
#                              their charge-rents, their ground pennies and
#                              their courtyard beds. Every denDuke, pledgedTo
#                              and priest id in it is REFUSED AT LOAD unless
#                              the owner's notables.json has it, so without the
#                              file in the context that refusal is proved by an
#                              absence.
#   actors/household.json      the canon household-size weights the ward's
#                              population is DERIVED from ({1:20,2:35,3:25,
#                              4:15,5:5}, the same numbers section 2.5 cites).
#                              Without it the ward silently falls back to a
#                              copy of those weights compiled into the code,
#                              and the claim that the population comes out of
#                              the owner's file would be untested.
!content/raws/compounds/**
!content/raws/actors/**

# --- re-admitted: the fixed origin-template sheets (#80) ---
# 8 KB, two files. devin.json and gabri.json are the DEVIN/GABRI halves of
# task #80's BG3/Divinity-Original-Sin-2-style origin templates -- hand-
# authored character sheets, not point-bought -- and CompanionTemplate::load
# (sim/companions.hpp) is silent about a missing file for the identical
# reason every other raws loader above is. Without these two files in the
# context, every companion case would either fail loudly (the honest
# outcome the suite actually got before this line existed) or, if the loader
# were ever made more permissive, pass against nothing -- the same empty-
# content trap this file's own header warns about for barks.json and the
# rest.
#
#   companions/devin.json   4 KB
#   companions/gabri.json   4 KB
!content/raws/companions/**

# --- and then trim build junk back out of the re-admitted paths ---
native/build/**
native/out/**
native/.cache/**
native/**/*.o
native/**/*.obj
native/**/*.exe
native/**/*.pdb
native/**/*.ilk
native/**/CMakeUserPresets.json
native/**/.DS_Store
